# Desktop React antipatterns

When editing `apps/desktop` React (`src/components`, `src/screens`), avoid these patterns. Prefer the folder layout and controllers documented in `desktop-react-components.mdc` and `desktop-react-screens.mdc`.

## 1. Heavy conditionals inside JSX

**Do not** nest long ternaries or multi-branch `condition ? … : condition ? …` trees inside a large `return`.

**Do:**

- Early returns in the view or a child
- Boolean flags from `useXController` (`empty`, `loading`, `hasCover`, `needsInit`)
- Small helper components for each branch (`DraftEmpty`, `ReviewPreviewBody`, `EditorMoreActions`)

```tsx
// Bad
return (
  <div>
    {loading ? <Spinner /> : items.length === 0 ? <Empty /> : items.map(/* … */)}
  </div>
);

// Good — flags / early return / child owns branches
if (loading) return <Spinner />;
if (empty) return <Empty />;
return <ItemList items={items} />;
```

## 2. Inline `.map()` in large JSX trees

**Do not** map lists (hits, drafts, comments, swatches, tree nodes, breadcrumbs) inline inside a 100+ line parent.

**Do:** extract list and/or item components under the same feature folder’s `components/`.

```tsx
// Bad — map buried in parent return
{drafts.map((d) => (
  <Card key={d.id}>{/* lots of row UI */}</Card>
))}

// Good
<DraftList drafts={drafts} onOpen={onOpen} />
```

Thin shells that only map → item component (`Tree` → `TreeNode`) are fine.

## 3. Oversized single-file components

**Do not** keep cover chrome + menus + pickers, or form state + effects + mode cards, in one large file.

**Do:**

- Folder-per-component: `Name/Name.tsx`, `index.ts`, `__specs__/`, optional `hooks/`, `components/`
- Lift local orchestration into `hooks/useNameController.ts`
- Keep screens thin: call only `useFeatureController`

```
Name/
  Name.tsx
  index.ts
  __specs__/Name.spec.tsx
  hooks/useNameController.ts   # when it owns state/effects/flags
  components/                  # list/item/empty subparts
```

## 4. God controller / flat bag of fields

**Do not** put page edit + threads + chrome/UI + keyboard + save in one 200+ line hook that returns 30+ flat fields.

**Do:** split into **domain subhooks**; the screen controller only composes and returns **namespaced groups**:

```ts
export function useEditorController(props) {
  const editor = useFormatter(...);
  const threads = useThreads(...);
  const comments = useComments({ ..., onThreadsRefresh: threads.onThreadsRefresh });
  const chrome = useEditorChrome(...);
  useKeyboardShortcuts({ onClose, editor, threads, comments, chrome });
  return { editor: { ...editor, commentsOn: comments.commentsOn }, threads, comments, chrome };
}
```

View: `const { editor, threads, comments, chrome } = useEditorController(props)`.

## 5. Exporting raw `setState`

**Do not** expose `setTitle`, `setOpenThread`, `setImageMap`, etc. to the screen.

**Do:** read-only state values + descriptive `on*` handlers (`onTitleChange`, `onThreadOpen`, `onCoverUpload`, …). Domain hooks own IPC side-effects (upload, reply, resolve, review send) — not inline in the screen.

```tsx
// Bad
onOpenThread={c.setOpenThread}
onUpload={async (file) => { /* IPC + setImageMap in JSX */ }}

// Good
onOpenThread={threads.onThreadOpen}
onUpload={editor.onImageUpload}
```

## 6. Cross-cutting keyboard / shortcuts

**Do not** keep large Escape-cascade or Cmd/Ctrl+S `keydown` effects inline in the feature orchestrator.

**Do:** extract `useKeyboardShortcuts` (or `useXShortcuts`) that receives the domain APIs it needs (`editor`, `threads`, `comments`, `chrome`, `onClose`). The orchestrator only calls the hook — it must not own the keydown body.

## 7. Domain split inside a domain

**Do not** grow a single “page” hook forever (title + save + comments gate + comment draft + …).

**Do:** further segment when concerns diverge — e.g. `useFormatter` (document body/title/save/frontmatter/images) vs `useComments` (`commentsOn` + new-comment draft modal). **Threads ≠ new-comment draft**: existing thread list/open/reply/resolve stays in `useThreads`; starting a selection comment belongs in `useComments`.

## 8. Magic / plain strings for status & modes

**Do not** scatter string literals for save status, lifecycle, body CSS classes, or mode checks (`"in_review"`, `"workflow-workspace"`, `"personal"`).

**Do:** use shared enums / `as const` maps in the feature (`screens/editor/enums.ts`, `screens/home/enums.ts`): `SaveStatus`, `LifecycleKind`, `ModalKind`, `NavKind`, `AccountMenuAction`, … Compare with enum members (`LifecycleKind.InReview`, `ModalKind.Init`); i18n keys can still interpolate the enum’s string value (`editor.lifecycle.${kind}`). Prefer importing shared types from `@slash-md/core` when they exist, with local enums matching those string values for comparisons. Dropdown / menu item `id`s that drive `onAction` switches belong in the same feature enums — not duplicated string literals in JSX and controllers.

## 9. One `onOpen` that hides two intents

**Do not** make a single `onOpen` both “open preserving query” and “open with cleared query” (or clear only from some call sites inline).

**Do:** name the intents — e.g. search palette `onOpen` (preserve) vs `onOpenCleared` (rail entry). Keyboard `/` and Cmd+K toggle should not surprise-clear; explicit search entry may clear.

